# 0-bootstrap

The chicken-and-egg layer. It adopts the resources the {{if .IsRoot}}seed step created by
hand{{else}}creation made{{end}}, {{if .IsRoot}}creates the dns layer identity, grants it the zones' administration in the
boot project and the boot identity what it needs on the organization's folder{{else}}creates the org layer identity, grants both identities what they need
on the organization's folder{{end}}, and makes the Cloud Build triggers that plan and apply every
layer of this repository. Nothing is granted at the organization node{{if .IsRoot}} but
the creating identity's Folder Creator, by which it makes a hosting
organization's folder there (step 7){{end}}: the folder is the whole of this
organization's reach. Its first apply is the {{if .IsRoot}}break-glass account's, from a
person's computer, before any layer identity can run{{else}}root organization's
creating identity's, in the creation, before any layer identity can run
("Created by the root organization" below){{end}}; from then on the
apply trigger applies it as the boot identity, like every layer ("The Cloud
Build triggers" below).

## What it creates

- Adopts, through `import` blocks: the organization's folder at the org root
  and the `terraform` folder under it, the
  boot project `{{.Prefix}}-boot-gbl-core-<suffix>` inside it, and the boot layer
  identity `{{.Prefix}}-boot-gbl-tofu`. From the first apply on they are ordinary
  managed resources.
{{- if .IsRoot}}
- The boot project's API set (Cloud Build, resource manager, Cloud DNS, IAM,
  IAM credentials, logging, Secret Manager, service usage, storage), its
  labels, and the deletion of its default compute service account.
- Role bindings on the organization's folder for the boot layer identity
  (`var.boot_layer_roles`) and on the boot project alone
  (`var.boot_project_roles`), and `roles/dns.admin` on the boot project alone
  for the dns layer identity, whose zones live there (`1-dns`).
- The dns layer identity `{{.Prefix}}-dns-gbl-tofu`, in the boot project.
- The two boot layers' plan identities `{{.Prefix}}-boot-gbl-plan` and
  `{{.Prefix}}-dns-gbl-plan`, in the boot project, with the reads on the
  organization's folder of `var.boot_plan_roles`, the boot project as their quota project, the
  planner's right to become them (`cloud-build.tf`), for the boot one the
  reads of the connection, the repository link and the triggers on the boot
  project (`roles/cloudbuild.connectionViewer`, `roles/cloudbuild.builds.viewer`,
  `cloud-build.tf`) and, for the dns one, the read of the infrastructure
  GitHub App's key (`github.tf`) and of the zones (`roles/dns.reader` on the
  boot project): a pull request plans each boot layer as its own read-only
  identity. A binding on the organization's folder is this layer's to make;
  nothing of the organization's is bound at the organization node.
- The custom role `projectUpdater` in the boot project
  (`resourcemanager.projects.update`), granted to the boot identity on it.
- `roles/billing.user` on the billing account for the boot identity, only when
  `manage_billing_iam` is true. See step 5.
- The Cloud Build side (`cloud-build.tf`): the GitHub connection
  `{{.ConnectionName}}` in the boot project, from the Cloud Build GitHub App's
  installation and the token secret's version `placement.json` records
  (`cloudBuildInstallationId`, `cloudBuildTokenSecretVersion`; "The Cloud
  Build GitHub authorization" below); the link of this repository
  ({{.InfrastructureRepository}}) under it; the read of the token secret for
  the boot project's Cloud Build service agent; the two runner identities
  `{{.Prefix}}-org-gbl-planner` and `{{.Prefix}}-org-gbl-applier` with their
  grants; and the two triggers, `{{.PlanTriggerName}}` on a pull request into
  `{{.GithubDefaultBranch}}` and `{{.ApplyTriggerName}}` on a push to it, whose
  steps are the trigger's own ("The Cloud Build triggers").
- The three GitHub Apps' key secrets in the boot project (`github.tf`), one
  per app for every bedrock organization in the Google Cloud organization:
  `{{.InfrastructureKeySecret}}`, `{{.DeployerKeySecret}}` and
  `{{.ReleaseKeySecret}}`, each with the alias `current` at the version
  `placement.json` pins, which every reader names; the custom role
  `secretPolicyAdmin`, which each hosting organization's boot identity holds
  on the three (`hostingOrganizations`); and the key operators' grants
