## The Cloud Build triggers

Every layer of this repository is planned on a pull request and applied on
its merge by two Cloud Build triggers in the boot project, rendered by
bedrock like the layers (`cloud-build.tf`). The plan trigger
(`{{.PlanTriggerName}}`) runs on every pull request into
`{{.GithubDefaultBranch}}`: it plans each layer whose `.tf`, `.tfvars` or lock
file the pull request changes, every layer when `.opentofu-version` changes,
side by side, each as that layer's plan identity, and posts each plan on the
pull request as the deployer GitHub App, one comment per layer kept current
across pushes. The apply trigger (`{{.ApplyTriggerName}}`) runs on every push
to `{{.GithubDefaultBranch}}`: it applies each layer changed since that layer's
last successful apply (a marker under `applied/` in the state bucket holds
the commit; a layer with no marker is applied), as that layer's apply
identity, in layer order ({{.LayerOrderProse}}), one at a time, stopping at
the first failure. A plan that fails only because it reads outputs the plan
of an earlier layer in the same pull request creates passes, "planned after"
that layer applies, since the merge applies that layer first (the root README,
"How the layers are applied").

The steps are the trigger's, so a pull request cannot change them; a pull
request changes only what the steps read. Each run is three steps for a
release pin and four for a commit pin: the first reads the bedrock pin off
`placement.json` in the checkout (`bedrockVersion` and, for a release,
`bedrockSha256`; `bedrock upgrade` moves it) and downloads the release's
binary, verified against the checksum, or for a commit checks with git that a
branch of `cccteam/ccc` reaches it (a commit no branch reaches stops the
run); for a commit pin a second step builds it with `go install`, where Go's
checksum database verifies it (a release pin renders no such step, so a
production organization's runs never pull the Go image); the next runs
`bedrock org check`, which refuses the run when a committed layer differs
from what the placement renders, holds a `.tf` file no render produces, or
holds an override file; the last runs `bedrock org plan` or `bedrock org
apply`. The steps follow the pin's kind at render time: `bedrock upgrade`
renders this layer with the pin it moves, and when the pin changes kind, a
release to a commit or a commit to a release, it applies this layer itself,
as the person running it, after `yes` typed back, since no trigger applies
this layer and the release shape's fetch step refuses a commit pin (no step
follows to build it). A pull request that reaches the plan trigger with a
commit pin while the triggers are in the release shape fails at the fetch
step, whose message says to apply this layer from that branch by hand. The
bedrock the run uses is the one the checkout pins, never one the trigger
names.

The trigger's steps lag one apply behind the repository. A change to them
is rendered by a new bedrock, planned and applied by a run on the old steps,
and the next run is the first on the new ones. Since the pin is read at run
time, a pull request that moves it already runs the new bedrock under the
old steps, so the one rule bedrock keeps is that a release works under the
previous release's steps. Both triggers pass `_BEDROCK_STEPS`, the shape of
their steps and the kind of pin they were rendered for, and `bedrock org
plan` and `apply` say in the run's log when they run under another shape.

To apply one layer again with no change to it{{if .IsRoot}}, start the apply trigger by hand
on the default branch with the layer{{else}}, which a registration's second
pass of `2-env` needs, start the apply trigger by hand on the default branch
with the layer, and for `2-env` the environment (without one, the three in
order){{end}}; in a terminal, as a person holding `roles/cloudbuild.builds.editor`
on the boot project:

```bash
gcloud builds triggers run {{.ApplyTriggerName}} --region={{.Primary.Name}} --project=<boot project> --branch={{.GithubDefaultBranch}} --substitutions={{.RerunExample}}
```

Cloud Build runs the builds of a trigger as they come; two applies of one
layer at once would contend for the layer's state lock, and the second
fails on it, so start a run by hand when none is running.

Who runs what, and why the first time works in order:

{{- if .IsRoot}}

- The plan trigger's builds run as the planner (`{{.Prefix}}-org-gbl-planner`),
  the apply trigger's as the applier (`{{.Prefix}}-org-gbl-applier`). Neither
  holds a layer's roles: each impersonates the layer identity of its kind for
  that layer's tofu (`roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator` on each identity:
  this layer grants it on its four identities, every identity a root
  organization has), so a plan runs with a plan identity's reads and an apply
  with the apply identity's writes. What each holds itself: the build logs,
  the read of its own build (its substitutions, the pull request, the
  commit), the connection's read token (the changed files are read through
  it), the two apps' keys, and, for the applier, the markers under `applied/`
  in the state bucket and the read of the folder's and the projects' IAM
  policies for the check's leftover report.
- IAM refuses a binding for a service account that does not exist, so each
  layer grants for the identities it creates. This layer grants for the boot
  identity, the dns identity and the two boot layers' plan identities
  (`{{.Prefix}}-boot-gbl-plan`, `{{.Prefix}}-dns-gbl-plan`), all of which exist
  when its grants apply; `1-dns` creates no identity.
- The first apply of this layer and the first apply of `1-dns` are the
  break-glass account's (steps 3 and 4 above, and `1-dns/README.md`), as the
  organization folder's administrator and with the roles step 1 grants. The bucket exists from step 1, so this layer's
  grants on it apply in step 3; `1-dns` grants nothing on it.
- From the first apply of this layer on, a push to `{{.GithubDefaultBranch}}`
  can apply this layer and `1-dns` through the apply trigger, and a pull
  request can plan them (their four identities and the runners' grants on
  them exist). Nothing follows: a root organization has no other layer.

Each identity's role set is completed by refusal: a layer applied as its
identity that is refused a permission gets the role that carries it, in
`var.boot_layer_roles` (or `var.boot_project_roles`, for a role Google binds on
a project and never on a folder) or, for the dns identity, `bootstrap.tf`'s
grant on the boot project; a plan of a boot layer, in `var.boot_plan_roles` or the dns
plan identity's grant there.
{{- else}}

- The plan trigger's builds run as the planner (`{{.Prefix}}-org-gbl-planner`),
  the apply trigger's as the applier (`{{.Prefix}}-org-gbl-applier`). Neither
  holds a layer's roles: each impersonates the layer identity of its kind for
  that layer's tofu (`roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator` on each identity:
  this layer grants it on its four identities, `1-org` on the twelve it
  creates), so a plan runs with a plan identity's reads and an
  apply with the apply identity's writes. What each holds itself: the build
  logs, the read of its own build (its substitutions, the pull request, the
  commit), the connection's read token (the changed files are read through
  it), the two apps' keys, and, for the applier, the markers under
  `applied/` in the state bucket and the read of the folder's and the projects'
  IAM policies for the check's leftover report.
- IAM refuses a binding for a service account that does not exist, so each
  layer grants for the identities it creates. This layer grants for the boot
  identity, the org identity and the two boot layers' plan identities
  (`{{.Prefix}}-boot-gbl-plan`, `{{.Prefix}}-org-gbl-plan`), all of which exist
  when its grants apply; `1-org` grants for the twelve identities of the six
  projects. `2-env` grants for the application identities it creates, with
  the bucket's policy authority `1-org` gives each environment layer identity.
- The first apply of this layer and the first apply of `1-org` are the
  root organization's creating identity's, in the creation ("Created by the
  root organization" above, and `1-org/README.md`), as the organization
  folder's administrator and with the roles it granted itself there. The
  bucket exists from the seed, so this layer's grants on it apply in that
  first apply; `1-org`'s grants apply in its first apply as the same identity,
  and from then on as the org identity, which holds the bucket's policy
  authority from here.
- From the first push on, a push to `{{.GithubDefaultBranch}}` applies every
  layer through the apply trigger, and a pull request plans them: every
  identity exists and `placement.json` names every project before the push.
  A run that touches a layer whose identity's project the placement does not
  record (`REPLACEME`) says so and stops.

Each identity's role set is completed by refusal: a layer applied as its
identity that is refused a permission gets the role that carries it, in
`var.boot_layer_roles` (or `var.boot_project_roles`, for a role Google binds on
a project and never on a folder), `var.org_layer_roles` or `1-org`'s
`layer_roles`; a
plan of a boot layer, in `var.boot_plan_roles`.
{{- end}}

## The infrastructure GitHub App

{{- if .IsRoot}}

`1-dns` rules this repository's default branch with the GitHub provider,
which needs a token with an organization owner's rights. In a run of the
triggers that token is an installation token of the infrastructure GitHub
App, minted in the run from its private key. An app beside the deployer app,
because widening the deployer app would give the plan comments those rights.
One GitHub organization holds one such app, shared by every bedrock
organization in it: where a hosting organization has it already, this
organization records the same App ID and installs nothing new. Once, by a
person:

1. On GitHub, as an owner of the `{{.GithubOrganization}}` organization: the
   organization's Settings, Developer settings, GitHub Apps, New GitHub App.
   Name it `{{.GithubOrganization}}-infrastructure`, give it any homepage URL,
   turn Webhook off. Repository permissions: Administration (read and write),
   Metadata (read); the provider adopts this repository and writes its
   ruleset on Administration. Where can this app be installed: only this
   account. Create it, and note the App ID on its settings page. A permission
   added later is accepted on the installation: the organization's Settings,
   Third-party Access, GitHub Apps, the app.
{{- else}}

`1-org` configures the applications' repositories with the GitHub provider,
which needs a token with an organization owner's rights. In a run of the
triggers that token is an installation token of the infrastructure GitHub
App, minted in the run from its private key. A third app beside the release
and deployer apps, because widening the deployer app would give every
application pipeline those rights. Once, by a person:

1. On GitHub, as an owner of the `{{.GithubOrganization}}` organization: the
   organization's Settings, Developer settings, GitHub Apps, New GitHub App.
   Name it `{{.GithubOrganization}}-infrastructure`, give it any homepage URL,
   turn Webhook off. Repository permissions: Actions (read), Administration
   (read and write), Metadata (read); the provider reads a repository's
   environments and their branch policies on the Actions permission and
   writes them on Administration; Contents (read and write), with which the
   root organization's creation pushes a created organization's rendered
   repository. Organization permissions: Administration (read and write),
   Variables (read and write) and Self-hosted runners (read and write); the
   second is for the organization's `CI_LARGE_RUNNER` variable, which the
   root organization sets when its placement names a larger runner
   (`ciLargeRunner`), and the third for the runner and its runner group, which
   it creates when the placement sizes it (`ciLargeRunnerSize`);
   without them that apply is refused 403. Where can this app be installed:
   only this account. Create it, and note the App ID on its settings page. A
   permission added later is accepted on the installation: the
   organization's Settings, Third-party Access, GitHub Apps, the app.
{{- end}}
2. On the app's settings page: Private keys, Generate a private key. The
   browser downloads a `.pem` file.
3. On the same page: Install App, on the organization, for all repositories.
{{- if .IsRoot}}
4. In a terminal, in this repository, as a key operator `placement.json`
   names (`keyOperators`), once this layer has made the secret: record the App
   ID in `placement.json` as `githubInfrastructureAppId`, then add the key,
   prove it and pin it:

   ```bash
   bedrock secret add github-infrastructure-key --from-file <the .pem file>
   bedrock org secrets check github-infrastructure-key <version>
   bedrock secret pin github-infrastructure-key <version>
   ```

   `add` puts the key into `{{.InfrastructureKeySecret}}` in the boot project
   as a new version and prints its number (1 for the first). `check` reads the
   version, signs as the app and asks GitHub, which answers for the app's own
   key alone. `pin` records the version as `githubInfrastructureKeyVersion` in
   `placement.json` and runs `bedrock org render`, which writes the alias
   `current` at that version into `github.tf`; the pull request's apply moves
   the alias, and every reader in every organization names the alias, never a
   number and never `latest`. Commit what they changed and delete the `.pem`
   file. Nobody edits the version by hand ("Rotating a GitHub App's key").

Until the App ID is recorded and the alias names a version, a run of the
triggers that plans or applies `1-dns` says so and stops; the Google side of
the layer is unaffected. A person applying `1-dns` by hand keeps using their
own token (`1-dns/README.md`).
{{- else}}
4. In `placement.json`: the App ID as `githubInfrastructureAppId`, then
   `bedrock org render`. The key itself lives in the root organization
   `{{.RootOrganization}}`'s boot project (`root` in `placement.json`), added,
   proven and pinned there by its key operators; a run of the triggers here
   reads the alias `current` of `{{.InfrastructureKeySecret}}`, which that
   organization pins, and nothing is added in this repository.

Until the App ID is recorded and the alias names a version, a run of the
triggers that plans or applies `1-org` says so and stops; the Google side of
the layer is unaffected. A person applying `1-org` by hand keeps using their
own token (`1-org/README.md`).
{{- end}}

## The GitHub Apps' keys

{{- if .IsRoot}}

This organization holds the private keys of the three GitHub Apps for every
bedrock organization in the Google Cloud organization: one secret per app in
the boot project (`github.tf`), `{{.InfrastructureKeySecret}}`,
`{{.DeployerKeySecret}}` and `{{.ReleaseKeySecret}}`, each version a key.
Every reader, this organization's runs of the triggers and the hosting
organizations' pipelines, names a secret's alias `current`, which
`placement.json` pins (`githubInfrastructureKeyVersion`,
`githubDeployerKeyVersion`, `githubReleaseKeyVersion`) and this layer's apply
moves: adding a version changes nothing by itself, and one pull request here
switches every reader at once. No key reaches GitHub, and nothing edits a
version number by hand.

Who does what, declared in `placement.json`:

- The key operators (`keyOperators`, IAM members: `user:`, `group:`) hold
  Secret Version Adder and Secret Accessor on the three secrets: they add a
  version and read it back for the check. Not the break-glass account.
- Each hosting organization (`hostingOrganizations`: its prefix and its boot
  project) has its boot identity granted `secretPolicyAdmin` on the three
  secrets, a custom role of this project that reads and sets a secret's IAM
  policy and nothing else (no payload, no version, no change to the secret).
  Its layers hand the right down to the identities that read a key, and that
  grant is the only reach a hosting organization has into this one. An
  organization is listed by `bedrock org create` before its creation, with
  the boot project the command names; the grant renders once the hand-off
  has recorded the folder, and in between the creating identity, which
  holds the same role on the four shared secrets (`bootstrap.tf`), grants
  the new boot identity as the seed, before the organization's `0-bootstrap`
  first applies (that organization's `0-bootstrap/README.md`, "Created by
  the root organization").
- The plan trigger's run posts each layer's plan on the pull request as the
  deployer app, and a run that plans or applies `1-dns` mints the
  infrastructure app's token; the runners read those two keys
  (`cloud-build.tf`). Nothing here reads the release key, which is held for
  the hosting organizations' release triggers.

The apps themselves, their permissions and their installations are made once
on GitHub (the root `README.md`, "The deployer GitHub App", and the hosting
organizations' for the release app); their App IDs are recorded here
(`githubInfrastructureAppId`, `githubDeployerAppId`, `githubReleaseAppId`),
since the check signs as the app. Each key, once, after this layer's first
apply has made the secrets, by a key operator:

```bash
bedrock secret add github-deployer-key --from-file <the deployer app's .pem file>
bedrock org secrets check github-deployer-key <version>
bedrock secret pin github-deployer-key <version>
```

The same with `github-release-key` and `github-infrastructure-key`. `add`
puts the key into the secret as a new version and prints its number; `check`
proves the version with GitHub; `pin` records the version in `placement.json`
and renders the alias into `github.tf`, and the pull request's apply moves
it. Delete the `.pem` file once it is pinned. Until a version is pinned, the
alias names none: a hosting organization's pipelines that read that key say
so and wait, and here the plans are in the runs' logs alone.

## Rotating a GitHub App's key

In one place, for every organization at once, with nothing taken down:

1. On GitHub, on the app's settings page: Private keys, Generate a private
   key. GitHub keeps the old key working beside the new one.
2. In a terminal, in this repository, as a key operator: `bedrock secret add
   <secret> --from-file <the .pem file>`, which adds the key as a new version
   and prints its number. Nothing reads it yet.
3. In the same terminal: `bedrock org secrets check <secret> <version>`, which
   reads the version, signs as the app and asks GitHub for the app itself.
   GitHub answers for the app's own key alone, so an answer proves the
   version and a refusal says the version is not the app's; nothing moves.
4. In the same terminal: `bedrock secret pin <secret> <version>`, then the
   pull request with what it changed. Its apply moves the alias `current` to
   the version, and every pipeline in every organization reads the new key
   from its next run. Rolling back is the same pull request the other way,
   while the old key exists on GitHub.
5. On GitHub, on the app's settings page: delete the old key once the next
   run has gone through clean.
{{- else}}

The three GitHub Apps' private keys live in the root organization
`{{.RootOrganization}}`'s boot project (`{{.RootProject}}`; `root` in
`placement.json`), one secret per app for every bedrock organization in the
Google Cloud organization: `{{.InfrastructureKeySecret}}`,
`{{.DeployerKeySecret}}` and `{{.ReleaseKeySecret}}`. Every reader here names
a secret's alias `current`, which that organization's placement pins and its
apply moves; nothing is added or pinned in this repository, `bedrock secret
add` and `pin` refuse here and name that organization, and a rotation there
switches every reader at once (that organization's `0-bootstrap/README.md`,
"Rotating a GitHub App's key"). No key reaches GitHub.

What this organization records is the apps' App IDs, which are not secrets:
`githubInfrastructureAppId` for the infrastructure app, `githubDeployerAppId`
for the deployer app, which the plan trigger's run posts each layer's plan on
the pull request as and every application's pipeline talks back on its pull
requests as (a deployment carrying the environment's URL, a comment, the
guard's refusals), and `githubReleaseAppId` for the release app, which each
application's release trigger cuts the application's releases as
(release-please, in Cloud Build as the application's release identity). The
apps themselves, their permissions and their installations are made once on
GitHub (the root `README.md`, "The two GitHub Apps"); a GitHub organization
that holds the root organization has them already, and this organization
records the same App IDs.

How the reads are granted (`github.tf`): the root organization gives this
organization's boot identity `secretPolicyAdmin` on the three secrets, a
custom role of its project that reads and sets a secret's IAM policy and
nothing else. This layer grants from it: the org identity's policy
administration of the deployer and release secrets, which `1-org` hands to
each environment layer identity so `2-env` can grant the applications'
identities their read; the org plan identity's read of the infrastructure
key; the runners' reads of the infrastructure and deployer keys
(`cloud-build.tf`); and both boot plan identities' read of the three secrets'
policies, which a plan of this layer or of `1-org` refreshes the grants
through. That is the only reach this organization has into that one.

Both grants came with the creation ("Created by the root organization"
above): `bedrock org create` listed this organization in the root
organization's `placement.json` (`hostingOrganizations`: the prefix and the
boot project, whose suffix the command chose) before the merge, and the
creating identity, which holds the same role on the four shared secrets,
granted the boot identity as the seed and then applied this layer with its
own policy administration of them. The hand-off recorded the folder, from
which that organization's `0-bootstrap` keeps the boot identity's grant
rendered. Nothing was granted by hand.

Until the App IDs are recorded, the plans are in the runs' logs alone and
the pipelines talk back through nothing; until the root organization has
pinned a key, the pipelines that read it say so and wait. Nothing else is
affected.
{{- end}}

## Recovery, by hand

{{- if .IsRoot}}

When the triggers cannot run (the connection broken, the repository
unreachable, a change that must be applied before it can merge), a person
applies from their own computer, signed in as the break-glass account: with
the Folder Admin it keeps on the organization's folder it grants itself, for
the run, the roles the layer's identity holds there (`variables.tf`), applies
the way the first applies ran (step 3 above, without the local state;
`1-dns/README.md`, "Applying"), and takes the grants off after. An apply by
hand leaves the layer's marker as it was, so the next push applies the layer
again: the plan is empty when the hand apply was complete.
{{- else}}

When the triggers cannot run (the connection broken, the repository
unreachable, a change that must be applied before it can merge), a person
applies from their own computer. For `2-env`, a member of the environment's
team group asks for two entitlements for the same short time: the layer
administrator, the environment layer identity's roles on the environment
project, and the layer state entitlement, the identity's slot in the state
bucket and the boot project as its quota project. The person then runs the
layer as themselves, with the identity's roles, on the same code as the
triggers, and the audit log names the person (`2-env/README.md`). This layer
and `1-org` have no entitlement: their recovery is the break-glass account's,
which grants itself, for the run, the roles the layer's identity holds on the
organization's folder (`variables.tf`) with the Folder Admin the creation
gave it there, applies from the layer's directory against the bucket as the
creation's first applies ran (`tofu init`, then `tofu apply`, with its own
sign-in and, for `1-org`, their own GitHub token), and takes the grants off
after. An apply by hand leaves the layer's marker as it was, so the next
push applies the layer again: the plan is empty when the hand apply was
complete.
{{- end}}

## Destroying the organization

{{- if .IsRoot}}

`bedrock org destroy` deletes the boot project, and with it everything the
layers made: the layer identities, the state bucket with both layers' states,
the connection and the triggers, the GitHub Apps' keys, and the zones with
their record sets. The folders and the custom roles go with it, and on GitHub
this repository's ruleset. The repository stays, with its code and pull
requests. Every domain delegated to this organization then points at servers
that answer nothing, so a root organization is destroyed once no domain is
delegated to it (`1-dns/README.md`). Google keeps a deleted project for 30
days, in which the console can restore it; its id never returns after.
{{- else}}

`bedrock org destroy` deletes every project of the organization, and with them
everything the layers and the applications made: the layer identities, the
state bucket with every layer's and every application's state and records, the
connection and the triggers, the GitHub Apps' keys, the registry and its
images, the Spanner instances and databases, the load balancer and the zone,
every environment's services, jobs, secrets, records and files. The folders,
the custom roles and the tags, the org policies, the entitlements and the audit
log sink go with them, and on GitHub the rulesets, the operations environments,
the runner group and the organization variable. The repositories stay, with
their code, pull requests and releases. Google keeps a deleted project for 30
days, in which the console can restore it; its id never returns after.
{{- end}}

It runs by hand, at a person's computer, signed in as the break-glass account
(`gcloud auth application-default login`), holding Owner on the organization's
folder for the run, which the Folder Admin it keeps there lets it grant itself
and which goes with the folder; with `GITHUB_TOKEN`
set to the infrastructure GitHub App's installation token or an organization
owner's token (gh's token serves when gh is signed in as one). It says what
goes, project by project, and asks for the prefix typed back before anything
runs; `--yes` stands in for it where there is no terminal, as in a disposable
organization's scripts.

{{- if .IsRoot}}

Then it destroys `1-dns` and `0-bootstrap`, in that order. `1-dns` first: this
repository and the zones with their record sets are taken out of its state
(the repository stays on GitHub; the zones go with the boot project, which
their `prevent_destroy` would otherwise refuse), then the destroy, which
deletes the ruleset. `0-bootstrap` in two steps: an apply with `destroy=true`,
the variable it declares for this command alone, records the boot project's
deletion policy as DELETE, records that no API is disabled on the destroy, and
lifts the folders' deletion protection, all three read by the provider from
the state and not from the configuration; then the destroy, tried again after
a pause while it fails, since a folder refuses for a while after its last
project's deletion. Its state lives in the bucket the boot project holds, so
the destroy runs on a copy of the layer with a local copy of the state,
pulled before the project goes. A layer whose state holds nothing is skipped,
so the command also finishes an organization whose first applies stopped
halfway. The command refuses a checkout rendered by an earlier bedrock, which
declares no `destroy` variable: `bedrock org render` first.
{{- else}}

Then it destroys `1-org` and `0-bootstrap`, in that order, each in two steps.
An apply with `destroy=true`, the variable both layers declare for this command
alone, records the projects' deletion policy as DELETE, records that no API is
disabled on the destroy (the project module disables each API it enabled before
deleting the project, and an API a resource outside the layer's state still uses,
Compute Engine's for `2-env`'s pull-request backend, refuses; the project's
deletion takes everything regardless), and lifts the folders' deletion
protection, all three read by the provider from the state and not from the
configuration. Then the destroy, tried again after a pause while it fails: a
folder refuses for a while after its last project's deletion, and a tag value
while a binding in a project being deleted still holds it, and the next attempt
gets past both. `1-org`'s GitHub repositories are taken out of its state
first. `0-bootstrap`'s state lives in the bucket the boot project holds, so its
destroy runs on a copy of the layer with a local copy of the state, pulled
before the project goes. A layer whose state holds nothing is skipped, so the
command also finishes an organization whose first applies stopped halfway. The
inner layers and the applications' stacks are never destroyed one by one:
deleting a project takes everything in it, whatever its resources' own
protections say.

When `2-net` registers domains (`registrations` in `2-net/terraform.tfvars`),
the network project is kept, with its folder and the organization's: a
registration cannot move to another project, and that project is a person's to
deal with (`1-org/README.md`, "Removing a project"). The command refuses a
checkout rendered by an earlier bedrock, which declares no `destroy` variable:
`bedrock org render` first.
{{- end}}

It ends with a line in `JOURNAL.md`. Starting over is a new seed (step 1, with
a new boot project), `bedrock org render` on its values, and the hand steps
again; the GitHub side (the organization, its machine account
`{{.GithubMachineAccount}}`, the apps and their installations) is as it was.
